---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'Odysseia';
const description =
  'A myth-inspired 3D adventure game developed by Gaucho Game Lab. I served as Puzzle Lead, designing and implementing systemic puzzle mechanics, levels, and gameplay systems in Unity.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>
      <!-- =========================================================
           HERO
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-brand">
          <img
            class="project-logo"
            src="/projects/odysseia/logo.webp"
            alt="Odysseia logo"
          />
        </div>

        <p class="eyebrow">
          GAME DEVELOPMENT · PUZZLE DESIGN · UNITY · C#
        </p>

        <h1>Odysseia</h1>

        <p class="lead">
          A myth-inspired 3D adventure game developed by Gaucho Game Lab.
          As <strong> Puzzle Lead</strong>, I designed and implemented systemic
          puzzle mechanics and mechanic-driven levels in Unity.
        </p>

        <div class="metadata">
          <div>
            <span>ROLE</span>
            <strong> Puzzle Lead</strong>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>TEAM</span>
            <strong> 15–20 Developers</strong>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>DURATION</span>
            <strong> Sep 2024 – Mar 2025</strong>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>TOOLS</span>
            <strong> Unity · C#</strong>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="hero-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/odysseia/tide-high.webp"
            alt="Odysseia gameplay showing the player navigating a tidal environment"
          />
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           01 OVERVIEW
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">01</p>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            My work on <em>Odysseia</em> focused on designing reusable puzzle
            systems that could grow in complexity while remaining readable to
            the player.
          </p>

          <p>
            I developed the mechanics, implemented them in Unity, designed
            levels around their interactions, and collaborated with the art
            team to improve visual communication and puzzle readability.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="contributions">
          <p class="card-label">MY CONTRIBUTIONS</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Designed and implemented the
              <strong> dynamic tidal system</strong> and
              <strong> time acceleration mechanic</strong>.
            </li>

            <li>
              Built a modular
              <strong> light puzzle system</strong> supporting redirection,
              dispersion, color fusion, and filtering.
            </li>

            <li>
              Designed mechanic-driven levels and conceptualized the
              <strong> Minotaur's Labyrinth</strong>.
            </li>

            <li>
              Collaborated with artists to improve mechanic readability,
              environmental communication, and level polish.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           02 TIDAL SYSTEM
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">02</p>
          <h2>Dynamic Tidal System</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            The tidal system turns the environment itself into part of the
            puzzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water levels rise and fall with the game's day–night cycle,
            dynamically changing which routes are available to the player.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="two-image-grid">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/tide-high.webp"
              alt="Odysseia environment during a higher tidal state"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Higher water levels cover obstacles and change which routes are
              traversable.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/tide-low.webp"
              alt="Odysseia environment during a lower tidal state"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Lower tides expose terrain, passages, and interactable areas.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="prose after-media">
          <p>
            During ebb tide, previously submerged spaces, objects, or routes
            can become accessible. During flood tide, rocks and reefs can
            become submerged, opening paths that would otherwise be blocked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The design shifts the player's reasoning from simply asking
            <strong> “Where should I go?”</strong>
            to also asking
            <strong> “When should I act?”</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           03 TIME CONTROL
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">03</p>
          <h2>Giving the Player Control Over Time</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="media-text">
          <figure>
            <!-- <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/time-speed.webp"
              alt="Time acceleration mechanic changing the day-night cycle"
            /> -->
            <video autoplay muted loop playsinline>
                <source src="/projects/odysseia/time-speed.webm" type="video/webm" />
            </video>

            <figcaption>
              The player accelerates the day–night cycle to manipulate tidal
              states directly.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="prose">
            <p class="intro">
              A time-driven environment creates an obvious design problem:
              waiting is not an interesting puzzle interaction.
            </p>

            <p>
              To solve this, I implemented a
              <strong> Time Acceleration System</strong> that allows players to
              deliberately advance the day–night cycle.
            </p>

            <p>
              This gives players agency over environmental state. Tides become
              something they can actively plan around and manipulate rather
              than something they simply wait for.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           04 LIGHT SYSTEM
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="content-section light-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">04</p>
          <h2>Progressive Light Puzzle System</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            I designed the light system around progressive complexity:
            introduce one understandable rule, then repeatedly expand what that
            rule can do.
          </p>

          <p>
            At its foundation, light travels from an emitter to a receiver.
            From this simple interaction, the mechanic expands into a larger
            system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="progression">
          <div class="progression-item">
            <span class="step-number">01</span>
            <h3>Emission</h3>
            <p>A light source activates a receiver.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="progression-item">
            <span class="step-number">02</span>
            <h3>Redirection</h3>
            <p>Relay nodes turn activation into path-building.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="progression-item">
            <span class="step-number">03</span>
            <h3>Dispersion</h3>
            <p>White light splits into RGB channels.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="progression-item">
            <span class="step-number">04</span>
            <h3>Fusion</h3>
            <p>Channels recombine into new color states.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="progression-item">
            <span class="step-number">05</span>
            <h3>Filtering</h3>
            <p>Color constraints create selective routing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="wide-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/odysseia/light-relay.webp"
            alt="Light beam redirected between puzzle nodes"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Relay nodes expand the basic emitter–receiver interaction into a
            path-building puzzle.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose after-media">
          <h3>From a Single Beam to a System</h3>

          <p>
            Relay nodes allow beams to be redirected across the environment.
            This transforms a simple activation mechanic into a routing
            problem, where players construct a path between multiple objects.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prisms then split white light into red, green, and blue channels,
            introducing branching paths and dependencies between simultaneous
            signals.
          </p>

          <p>
            At higher complexity levels, beams can be filtered or recombined.
            New puzzle challenges therefore emerge from combinations of rules
            the player has already learned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="two-image-grid">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/light-filter.webp"
              alt="Light puzzle using a colored filtering surface"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Colored surfaces selectively filter channels, introducing
              constraint-based routing.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure>
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/light-complex.webp"
              alt="Complex multi-room light routing puzzle"
            />

            <figcaption>
              Multiple beams, receivers, and filters allow puzzle logic to
              extend across larger spaces.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           05 LABYRINTH
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">05</p>
          <h2>Minotaur's Labyrinth</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro">
            The Minotaur's Labyrinth explores how a single puzzle system can
            shape the structure of an entire level rather than just an
            individual room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maze design creates a tension between exploration and guidance.
            A short solution path may cause players to skip much of the space,
            while a long forced route can make the maze feel overly linear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="concept-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/odysseia/labyrinth-concept.webp"
            alt="Early concept sketch of the Minotaur's Labyrinth showing red green and blue light routes"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Early concept sketch for the Minotaur's Labyrinth. RGB light routes
            divide the maze into interconnected regions and structure player
            traversal.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose after-media">
          <p>
            I used <strong> light dispersion</strong> as the structural backbone
            of the labyrinth.
          </p>

          <p>
            White light is divided into red, green, and blue routes, naturally
            separating the maze into three major regions. Each region contains
            a focused set of puzzles with enough depth to reward exploration
            without forcing the player into one excessively long path.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cross-region interactions reconnect the spaces. A beam originating
            in one region can become necessary for solving a puzzle in another,
            encouraging players to understand the labyrinth as one connected
            system rather than several isolated rooms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Persistent beams also become a form of environmental communication.
            Solved routes remain visible in the space, allowing the level
            itself to record progress and suggest unexplored possibilities.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           06 TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="technical-section">
        <div class="technical-inner">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <p class="section-number">06</p>
            <h2>Technical Implementation</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="prose">
            <p class="intro">
              The light puzzle system was implemented in Unity using C# with a
              modular, event-driven architecture.
            </p>

            <p>
              One of my main implementation goals was to separate
              <strong> light propagation</strong> from
              <strong> puzzle reactions</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <figure class="architecture-diagram">
            <img
              src="/projects/odysseia/light-architecture.webp"
              alt="Architecture diagram of the Odysseia light puzzle system"
            />

            <figcaption>
              System architecture showing how emitters, beams, receivers, and
              independent gameplay events communicate.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="technical-grid">
            <article>
              <p class="card-label">ARCHITECTURE</p>

              <h3>Event-Driven Modularity</h3>

              <p>
                Light receivers emit activation and deactivation events instead
                of directly controlling gameplay objects.
              </p>

              <p>
                Independent components can respond by opening doors, activating
                secondary beams, rotating objects, or triggering other gameplay
                behavior.
              </p>

              <p>
                This keeps the core light system independent from the specific
                objects that react to it.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article>
              <p class="card-label">EXTENSIBILITY</p>

              <h3>Specialized Receivers</h3>

              <p>
                A shared <code>LightReceiver</code> implementation handles
                common behavior such as light dependencies, color aggregation,
                activation state, and downstream propagation.
              </p>

              <p>
                Specialized receiver types extend that behavior only where a
                mechanic requires different interpretation of incoming light.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>

          <div class="code-concepts">
            <div>
              <code>LightReceiver</code>

              <p>
                Shared receiver logic for incoming light, activation, and
                propagation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <code>FilterLightReceiver</code>

              <p>
                Selectively passes color channels to support filtering-based
                puzzle constraints.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <code>SpecificLightReceiver</code>

              <p>
                Activates only when incoming light matches a required target
                color.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- =========================================================
           07 TAKEAWAYS
      ========================================================== -->
      <section class="content-section final-section">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="section-number">07</p>
          <h2>Takeaways</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p class="intro takeaway-lead">
            The project taught me to design game systems across multiple scales —
            from reusable C# components to puzzle rooms and whole-level structure.
            </p>

          <p>
            The light system demonstrated how a mechanic can gain depth through
            combinations and extensions, while the tidal system explored how
            changing environmental state can create another dimension of
            reasoning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Working as part of a larger team also required clear communication
            across disciplines so that gameplay logic, level design, and visual
            presentation supported the same player experience.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  /* ============================================================
     PAGE
     ============================================================ */

  main {
    width: min(1280px, calc(100% - 2.5rem));
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     SHARED TYPOGRAPHY
     ============================================================ */

  .eyebrow,
  .section-number,
  .card-label,
  .step-number {
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .eyebrow,
  .section-number,
  .card-label {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     HERO
     ============================================================ */

  .hero {
    padding: 5.25rem 0 7rem;
  }

  .hero-brand {
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  .project-logo {
    width: min(390px, 66%);
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
  }

  h1 {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 0 1.2rem;

    font-size: clamp(4rem, 9vw, 7.5rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
  }

  .lead {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0;

    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.1vw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.58;

    opacity: 0.82;
  }

  .metadata {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);

    max-width: 1000px;

    gap: 2rem;

    margin-top: 2.8rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;

    border-top: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.28);
  }

  .metadata div {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .metadata span {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .metadata strong {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .hero-image {
    margin: 3.2rem 0 0;
  }

  .hero-image img {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 7.5;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;

    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     GENERAL SECTIONS
     ============================================================ */

  .content-section {
    padding: 6.25rem 0;

    border-top: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.22);
  }

  .section-heading {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 74px minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: baseline;

    margin-bottom: 2.7rem;
  }

  .section-heading h2 {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0;

    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4vw, 3.8rem);
    line-height: 1.06;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     TEXT
     ============================================================ */

  .prose {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin-left: 74px;
  }

  .prose p {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.82;
  }

  .prose p + p {
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
  }

  .prose .intro {
    font-size: clamp(1.24rem, 2vw, 1.42rem);
    line-height: 1.62;
  }

  .prose h3 {
    margin-top: 0;

    font-size: 1.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .after-media {
    margin-top: 3.8rem;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     CONTRIBUTIONS
     ============================================================ */

  .contributions {
    max-width: 900px;

    margin: 3.2rem 0 0 74px;
    padding: 2.1rem 2.3rem;

    background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.095);

    border: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.2);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .contributions .card-label {
    font-weight: 800;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .contributions ul {
    margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
  }

  .contributions li {
    margin: 0.9rem 0;
    padding-left: 0.15rem;

    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.68;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     FIGURES
     ============================================================ */

  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;

    display: block;

    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  figcaption {
    max-width: 920px;

    margin-top: 0.7rem;

    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.55;

    opacity: 0.62;
  }

  .wide-image {
    margin-top: 3.2rem;
  }

  .two-image-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));

    gap: 1.5rem;

    margin-top: 3.8rem;
  }

  .two-image-grid img {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     TIME CONTROL
     ============================================================ */

  .media-text {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.55fr) minmax(320px, 0.85fr);

    gap: 3.5rem;

    align-items: center;
  }

  .media-text .prose {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .media-text figure img {
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     LIGHT PROGRESSION
     ============================================================ */

  .light-section {
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .progression {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));

    gap: 0.75rem;

    margin: 3.25rem 0 3.5rem;
    padding: 1.15rem;

    background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.065);

    border: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.14);
    border-radius: 16px;
  }

  .progression-item {
    position: relative;

    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 175px;
    padding: 1.3rem 1.1rem;

    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.62);

    border: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.18);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .progression-item:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: "→";

    position: absolute;

    top: 50%;
    right: -0.78rem;

    transform: translate(50%, -50%);

    z-index: 2;

    font-size: 1.05rem;

    opacity: 0.28;
  }

  .step-number {
    display: block;

    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;

    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  .progression-item h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;

    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;

    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .progression-item p {
    margin: 0;

    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.45;

    opacity: 0.68;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     LABYRINTH
     ============================================================ */

  .concept-image {
    width: 100%;

    margin-top: 3.2rem;
  }

  .concept-image img {
    width: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;

    background: white;

    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .concept-image figcaption {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     TECHNICAL SECTION
     ============================================================ */

  .technical-section {
    width: 100vw;

    margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw);

    padding: 7rem 0;

    background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.075);

    border-top: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.16);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.16);
  }

  .technical-inner {
    width: min(1280px, calc(100% - 2.5rem));
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .architecture-diagram {
    margin-top: 3.4rem;
  }

  .architecture-diagram img {
    width: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;

    background: white;

    border: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.12);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .technical-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));

    gap: 1.75rem;

    margin: 3.5rem 0 0 74px;
  }

  .technical-grid article {
    padding: 2.15rem;

    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.38);

    border: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.2);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .technical-grid .card-label {
    opacity: 0.65;
  }

  .technical-grid h3 {
    margin: 0.65rem 0 1.1rem;

    font-size: 1.4rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .technical-grid p {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
  }

  .code-concepts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));

    gap: 1.15rem;

    margin: 1.6rem 0 0 74px;
  }

  .code-concepts > div {
    padding: 1.45rem;

    background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08);

    border: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.12);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .code-concepts code {
    display: block;

    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;

    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-weight: 700;

    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .code-concepts p {
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0;

    font-size: 0.87rem;
    line-height: 1.55;

    opacity: 0.66;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     TAKEAWAYS
     ============================================================ */

  .final-section {
    padding-top: 6.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 9rem;
  }

  .final-section .prose {
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .final-section .intro {
    margin-bottom: 1.7rem;

    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.2vw, 1.55rem);
    line-height: 1.58;
  }

  .final-section .prose p:not(.intro) {
    opacity: 0.86;
  }
  .takeaway-lead {
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    }

  /* ============================================================
     TABLET
     ============================================================ */

  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .progression {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .progression-item {
